#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# primitive_root

#% text
# `primitive_root(n)` gives the smallest primitive root modulo `n`, a number whose powers give every residue prime to `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Primitive roots exist for `n` = 2, 4, `p^k` and `2*p^k` with `p` an odd prime; for other `n` the call stays unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
primitive_root(7)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
primitive_root(23)
#% output
#= 5

#% cell
primitive_root(50)
#% output
#= 27

#% cell
primitive_root(12)
#% output
#= primitive_root(12)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Its order is `euler_phi(n)`:

#% cell
[multiplicative_order(primitive_root(41), 41), euler_phi(41)]
#% output
#= [40, 40]

#% text
# Its powers run through every residue prime to `n`:

#% cell
sort(table(power_mod(3, k, 7), k, 6))
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `multiplicative_order`, `euler_phi`, `power_mod`, `carmichael_lambda`
